  "worked_solution": "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$1$\n$1$\n$1$\n$-5$\n$3$\n\n \n\n$\u2193$\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x-1{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$",
